      },
      "text": "## BFS — Layer by Layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "02-TRAVERSAL.md#013",
      "path": [
        "02-TRAVERSAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:graph-algorithms:02-traversal, git-history:graph-algorithms:02-traversal]",
        "canonical_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "concepts": "[BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components, cycle detection]",
        "current_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:graph-algorithms:traversal",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "graph-algorithms",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[graph traversal]",
        "section": "mathematics-physics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Graph Traversal",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "BFS dequeues a vertex, enqueues its undiscovered neighbors, and never revisits.\nBecause the queue is FIFO, vertices come out in non-decreasing distance from the\nsource — so the first time you reach a vertex is via a fewest-edges path."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-TRAVERSAL.md#014",
      "path": [
        "02-TRAVERSAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:graph-algorithms:02-traversal, git-history:graph-algorithms:02-traversal]",
        "canonical_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "concepts": "[BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components, cycle detection]",
        "current_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:graph-algorithms:traversal",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "graph-algorithms",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[graph traversal]",
        "section": "mathematics-physics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Graph Traversal",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n  Graph (undirected):        BFS from s, queue trace:\n                             ----------------------------------------\n       s --- a --- d        dequeue  discovers   dist set\n       |           |        s        a, b        a=1, b=1\n       b --- c ----+        a        d           d=2\n                            b        c           c=2\n                            c        (a,b seen)  -\n                            d        (a seen)    -\n                            => order s,a,b,c,d ; dist[d]=2 via s-a-d"
    },
    {
      "id": "02-TRAVERSAL.md#015",
      "path": [
        "02-TRAVERSAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:graph-algorithms:02-traversal, git-history:graph-algorithms:02-traversal]",
        "canonical_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "concepts": "[BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components, cycle detection]",
        "current_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:graph-algorithms:traversal",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "graph-algorithms",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[graph traversal]",
        "section": "mathematics-physics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Graph Traversal",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "LAYERS:   L0 = {s}   L1 = {a, b}   L2 = {c, d}\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "02-TRAVERSAL.md#016",
      "path": [
        "02-TRAVERSAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:graph-algorithms:02-traversal, git-history:graph-algorithms:02-traversal]",
        "canonical_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "concepts": "[BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components, cycle detection]",
        "current_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:graph-algorithms:traversal",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "graph-algorithms",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[graph traversal]",
        "section": "mathematics-physics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Graph Traversal",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Correctness (why first-discovery = shortest):** BFS maintains the invariant\nthat the queue holds vertices of at most two consecutive distances, dequeued in\nnon-decreasing order. When v is first dequeued, every shorter path would have\ndiscovered it earlier; hence `dist[v]` is exactly the hop-distance. Edges within\na layer or to an already-seen vertex are ignored — they cannot shorten anything."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-TRAVERSAL.md#017",
      "path": [
        "02-TRAVERSAL.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:graph-algorithms:02-traversal, git-history:graph-algorithms:02-traversal]",
        "canonical_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "concepts": "[BFS, DFS, topological sort, connected components, cycle detection]",
        "current_path": "graph-algorithms/02-TRAVERSAL.md",
        "id": "maxim:graph-algorithms:traversal",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "graph-algorithms",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[graph traversal]",
        "section": "mathematics-physics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Graph Traversal",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "> Old-world bridge: BFS is the unweighted special case of Dijkstra (all weights =\n> 1). A FIFO queue *is* a priority queue when every key increments by exactly one\n> — which is why a deque handles the 0/1-weight case (0-1 BFS) without a heap."
    },
